# Intent: The face is neutral by default (white on black, the classic look) and its style and colour are saved settings, not a reflash (features.md, Faces and look; decision 22). maipai_face_core.h is plain C: three styles (classic, big_eyes, minimal), a colour of none, one of seven named colours or a custom #rrggbb that is refused when too dark to see on the black screen (the face can never be made to vanish), the hours of the time-of-day bands, the language for the clock face (en or es), a versioned and checked save blob that falls back to the plain face when damaged, and plain error words that say what to do. maipai_face.cc keeps the settings in the unit's own flash (NVS namespace mp_face), draws them on the default skin (DefaultAvatar::setColors and setEyeStyleOffset, added with matching setColors on the eyes, mouth and speech bubble), and adds MCP tools self.face.set_config (one JSON object, partial update, nothing changes unless every field is valid) and self.face.get_config (settings plus the face features this build has). Behind CONFIG_MAIPAI_FACE_CONFIG (default y); off, none of it is compiled. The later face patches (F02 to F06) read the same settings.
# Placement: unit (the face is drawn and its settings are kept on the robot; the host half is host/maipai_body/faces.py)
# Upstream: firmware/main/stackchan/avatar/skins/default/ and firmware/main/hal/board/stackchan_display.cc of the StackChan tree (MIT); new files hal/maipai_face.{h,cc} and hal/maipai_face_core.h
# Check after a flash: call self.face.set_config with {"colour":"mint","style":"big_eyes"}: the face turns mint with larger eyes at once and stays so after a reboot; {"colour":"#101010"} answers ok:false with the too-dark words and changes nothing; {"colour":"none","style":"classic"} gives the plain white face back.
